<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>{{ title }}</title>
</head>
<body>

<section class="title-page" style="page: title;">
  <div class="title-band"></div>
  <h1 class="report-title">{{ title }}</h1>
  <p class="report-subtitle">Threat Model Report</p>
  <hr class="title-rule">
  <table class="title-meta">
    <tr><th>Date</th><td>{{ date }}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Author</th><td>{{ author.get('name', '') }}{% if author.get('role') %} ({{ author.role }}){% endif %}</td></tr>
    <tr><th>Business Criticality</th><td>{{ business_criticality | capitalize }}</td></tr>
  </table>
</section>

<section class="toc" style="page: toc;">
  <h1>Table of Contents</h1>
  <ul class="toc-list">
    <li class="toc-l0"><a href="#methodology">Part I: Application Profile</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#methodology"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#methodology">Threagile Methodology</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#methodology"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#executive-summary">Executive Summary</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#executive-summary"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#data-flow-diagram">Data-Flow Diagram</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#data-flow-diagram"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#data-asset-diagram">Data Asset Diagram</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#data-asset-diagram"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#security-requirements">Security Requirements</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#security-requirements"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#abuse-cases">Abuse Cases</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#abuse-cases"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#open-questions">Open Questions</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#open-questions"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#model-failures">Potential Model Failures</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#model-failures"></a></li>

    <li class="toc-l0"><a href="#impact-methodology">Part II: Application Risks</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#impact-methodology"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#impact-methodology">Risk Methodology</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#impact-methodology"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#impact-analysis">Severity Analysis of {{ remaining_finding_count }} Open Risks in {{ remaining_categories | length }} Categories</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#impact-analysis"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#accepted-risks">Severity Analysis of {{ accepted_finding_count }} Accepted Risks in {{ accepted_categories | length }} Categories</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#accepted-risks"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#closed-risks">Severity Analysis of {{ closed_finding_count }} Closed Risks in {{ closed_categories | length }} Categories</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#closed-risks"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#risks-by-category">Identified Risks by Vulnerability Category</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#risks-by-category"></a></li>
    {% for group in all_domain_groups %}
    <li class="toc-l2"><a href="#domain-{{ group.slug }}">{{ group.domain }}</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#domain-{{ group.slug }}"></a></li>
    {% endfor %}

    <li class="toc-l0"><a href="#tag-listing">Part III: Application Components</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#tag-listing"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#tag-listing">Tag Listing</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#tag-listing"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#data-assets">Data Assets</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#data-assets"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#technical-assets">Technical Assets</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#technical-assets"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#out-of-scope-assets">Out of Scope Assets</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#out-of-scope-assets"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#trust-boundaries">Trust Boundaries</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#trust-boundaries"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#shared-runtimes">Shared Runtimes</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#shared-runtimes"></a></li>
    <li class="toc-l1"><a href="#disclaimer">Disclaimer</a><span class="toc-fill"></span><a class="toc-page" href="#disclaimer"></a></li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- PART 1: APPLICATION PROFILE                                  -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<section id="methodology" style="page: content;">
  <h1>Part I: Application Profile</h1>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Threagile Methodology</h2>
  <p>This report was produced using <strong>Threagile</strong>, an open-source, model-driven threat
  modeling engine. The system's architecture, technical assets, trust boundaries, data flows, and
  data assets, is described as structured data rather than drawn free-hand, and Threagile analyzes
  that model against a library of risk rules to systematically identify where the architecture
  doesn't meet expected security properties. This model's own custom risk-injection engine extends
  that analysis with additional risk categories built for this organization's specific compliance
  and hardening requirements.</p>
</section>

<section id="executive-summary" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Executive Summary</h2>
  <p>What this system does, how it's built, and where its identified risks currently stand.</p>

  <h2>Business Criticality</h2>
  <p>This model is rated <strong>{{ business_criticality | capitalize }}</strong>.</p>
  <table class="asset-table">
    <tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Archive</td><td>Legacy or read-only reference system. Outage causes no operational impact.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Operational</td><td>Administrative or back-office tool. Outage is a minor inconvenience.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Important</td><td>Required for specific project execution. Outage degrades a specific group.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Critical</td><td>Essential daily enterprise tool. Outage causes widespread productivity disruption.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mission-Critical</td><td>Warfighting or core operational system. Outage causes immediate mission failure.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Application Overview</h2>
  <p>{{ management_summary_comment }}</p>

  <table class="summary-counts">
    <tr>
      <th>Severity</th>
      {% for status in ['unchecked', 'in-progress', 'in-discussion', 'mitigated', 'accepted', 'false-positive'] %}
      <th>{{ status | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</th>
      {% endfor %}
      <th>Total</th>
    </tr>
    {% for sev in severity_order %}
    {% set by_status = stats.get(sev, {}) %}
    <tr>
      <td class="severity-{{ sev }}">{{ sev | capitalize }}</td>
      {% for status in ['unchecked', 'in-progress', 'in-discussion', 'mitigated', 'accepted', 'false-positive'] %}
      <td>{{ by_status.get(status, 0) }}</td>
      {% endfor %}
      <td>{{ by_status.values() | sum }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>

  {% if business_overview.description %}
  <h2>Business Overview</h2>
  {% for para in business_overview.description.split('\n') if para.strip() %}<p>{{ para.strip() }}</p>{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}
  {% if technical_overview.description %}
  <h2>Technical Overview</h2>
  {% for para in technical_overview.description.split('\n') if para.strip() %}<p>{{ para.strip() }}</p>{% endfor %}
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="data-flow-diagram" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Data-Flow Diagram</h2>
  <p>This diagram visually depicts every technical asset, trust boundary, and communication link contained in the application model. 
  Small icons in a node's or boundary's corner call out specific application components or
  cloud services where tagged (see Tag Listing). This page is a fixed-size overview. For a
  full-resolution, zoomable version, see the accompanying <code>data-flow-diagram.html</code>
  (or <code>.svg</code>) file included alongside this report.</p>
  <div class="diagram">{{ data_flow_diagram | safe }}</div>
</section>

<section id="data-asset-diagram" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Data Asset Diagram</h2>
  <p>This diagram maps each data asset to every technical asset that sends, receives, or stores it. Both data
  assets and technical assets are shaded by their own Confidentiality level (see Data Assets). A
  solid edge indicates that the data asset is stored by that technical asset whereas a dashed edge depicts it is
  processed by or transits through that asset without being persisted there. This page is a
  fixed-size overview. For a full-resolution, zoomable version, see the accompanying
  <code>data-asset-diagram.html</code> (or <code>.svg</code>) file included alongside this
  report.</p>
  <div class="diagram">{{ data_asset_diagram | safe }}</div>
</section>

<section id="security-requirements" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Security Requirements</h2>
  <p>This section covers the mandatory controls and constraints for the application model. Entries are sourced from
  OWASP ASVS/AISVS or NIST SP 800-53, categorized by security domain, and rendered as tables.</p>
  {% for group in security_requirement_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for req in group.entries %}
  <h3 class="category-heading">{{ req.title }}</h3>
  {% if req.rows %}
  <table class="svs-table">
    <thead><tr><th class="svs-num">#</th><th>Description</th><th class="svs-level">Level</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in req.rows %}
      <tr><td class="svs-num">{{ row.num }}</td><td>{{ row.description }}</td><td class="svs-level level-{{ row.level }}">{{ row.level }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {% else %}
  <p>{{ req.text }}</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="abuse-cases" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Abuse Cases</h2>
  <p>This section describes misuse scenarios in the application model, which includes concrete attacker
  goals the architecture and its stated Security Requirements were designed against. Entries are categorized
  by the same domain outlined in the previous section.
  Any tailored entries not recognized under a domain fall under Uncategorized.</p>
  {% for group in abuse_case_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for ac_title, ac_text in group.entries %}
  <h3 class="category-heading">{{ ac_title }}</h3>
  <p>{{ ac_text }}</p>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="open-questions" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Open Questions</h2>
  <p>This section describes how the modeled application implements its security requirements through a
  question and answer format. The same domain categorization continues as laid out in previous sections.
  Any tailored entries not associated with a domain fall under Uncategorized.</p>
  {% for group in question_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for q_title, q_text in group.entries %}
  <h3 class="category-heading">{{ q_title }}</h3>
  <p>{{ q_text }}</p>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="model-failures" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Potential Model Failures</h2>
  <p>This section describes model failure findings from Threagile's model-consistency rules such as unreferenced or unnecessary assets,
  or incomplete boundary/link content. This does not indicate an architecture risk, but rather flags
  problems in how the model itself was authored and should be resolved by correcting the model,
  not by adding mitigating controls.</p>
  {% if model_failure_categories %}
  {% for cat in model_failure_categories %}
  <h2>{{ cat.title }}</h2>
  {% for f in cat.findings %}
  <div class="finding">
    <h3><span class="severity-badge severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | upper }}</span> {{ f.title }}</h3>
    <p><strong>Asset:</strong> {{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</p>
    {% if f.action %}<p><strong>Action:</strong> {{ f.action | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
    {% if f.mitigation %}<p><strong>Mitigation:</strong> {{ f.mitigation | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No model-failure findings in this run.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- PART 2: APPLICATION RISKS                                    -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<section id="impact-methodology" style="page: content;">
  <h1>Part II: Application Risks</h1>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Risk Methodology</h2>
  <p>This section describes the methodology in how each risk is categorized. Every finding gets a Severity rating on a five-level scale:
  <span class="severity-low">Low</span> &lt; <span class="severity-medium">Medium</span> &lt;
  <span class="severity-elevated">Elevated</span> &lt; <span class="severity-high">High</span> &lt;
  <span class="severity-critical">Critical</span>, and is shown throughout this report via the colored
  Severity column and badges. Severity is always derived from a Likelihood score and an Impact
  score for that finding, but how those two scores get computed depends on where the finding came
  from. This report draws on two separate rule sources, and each scores its findings differently:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Threagile's own built-in risk categories</strong> get their Severity computed by
    Threagile itself, using this report's own custom-built engine (<code>jessestarkey/threagile</code>),
    a fork of upstream Threagile that edits both the Severity formula and most individual rules'
    scoring. Each rule starts from its own author-judged baseline Likelihood and Impact. Most rules
    then nudge that baseline using the same per-asset adjustment the custom categories below use:
    Likelihood gets a Relative Attacker Attractiveness (RAA) and internet-reachability adjustment,
    and Impact gets narrowed to the single CIA dimension the finding's STRIDE category actually
    threatens where that fits (kept broader where a category's own documented Impact genuinely
    spans more than one dimension). A minority of rules -- checks for missing model elements or
    whole-model issues with no single asset to adjust for -- keep a flat baseline instead. Threagile
    then combines the two scores arithmetically: each Likelihood and Impact level carries a
    Fibonacci-spaced weight (1, 2, 3, 5 -- not a linear 1-4) rather than equal steps, since the real-world jump in
    exploitability or consequence from the third tier to the fourth is bigger than the jump from the first to the
    second. The two weights are then multiplied, and the product is bucketed into a
    Severity level, shown below.
    <table class="severity-matrix">
      <caption>Threagile's built-in Severity matrix</caption>
      <tr><th></th><th>Low (1)</th><th>Medium (2)</th><th>High (3)</th><th>Very-High (5)</th></tr>
      <tr><th>Unlikely (1)</th><td class="severity-low">Low</td><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Possible (2)</th><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Likely (3)</th><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-high">High</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Very-Likely (5)</th><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-critical">Critical</td></tr>
    </table>
    <br>
    </li>
    <li><strong>The model's custom risk-injection categories</strong> get their
    Severity computed by an independently-maintained scoring rule rather than calling Threagile's own. Each custom
    category starts from an author-judged baseline Likelihood and baseline Impact that is grounded in
    CWE or OWASP data where a mapping exists and expert judgment where it doesn't. Those
    baselines are then adjusted for the specific asset each finding landed on. Likelihood gets +1 rating increase
    if the asset's own RAA score is at or above 40%, and another +1 if the asset is
    internet-exposed via the <code>net:internet-reachable</code> tag. RAA is computed
    independently here, replicating Threagile's own RAA formula (asset and data sensitivity, data
    volume, a technology-type multiplier, and a "pivoting" effect where an asset inherits some
    attractiveness from whatever it can reach). Impact gets a -1, 0, or +1 adjustment from
    the asset's own rating on whichever specific CIA dimension the finding's STRIDE category
    threatens, rather than a flat maximum across all three. Impact gets an additional plus +1 if the model's overall
    Business Criticality is Critical or Mission-Critical, but only when the asset's own
    CIA-driven adjustment hasn't already earned that same +1, to prevent a duplicative impact adjustment.
    The two scores then combine through the same Fibonacci-weighted product-and-threshold rule as Threagile's own
    built-in path above, shown below -- the two paths are kept independently computed and maintained by design (see
    <code>docs/risk-methodology.md</code>), even where, as here, they currently land on the same matrix shape.
    <table class="severity-matrix">
      <caption>Custom-category Severity matrix</caption>
      <tr><th></th><th>Low (1)</th><th>Medium (2)</th><th>High (3)</th><th>Very-High (5)</th></tr>
      <tr><th>Unlikely (1)</th><td class="severity-low">Low</td><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Possible (2)</th><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Likely (3)</th><td class="severity-medium">Medium</td><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-high">High</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Very-Likely (5)</th><td class="severity-elevated">Elevated</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-high">High</td><td class="severity-critical">Critical</td></tr>
    </table>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Though the two paths are computed independently and can diverge for a similar-looking finding (they consult
  different Likelihood/Impact inputs even where the weighting and bucketing rule is now shared), both reach Severity
  the same general way: Likelihood and Impact combine through a fixed rule rather than a per-finding judgment call.</p>
</section>

<section id="impact-analysis" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Severity Analysis of {{ remaining_finding_count }} Open Risks in {{ remaining_categories | length }} Categories</h2>
  <p>This section covers Open risk items, which are marked unchecked, in-progress, or in-discussion.
  These are risks that have been neither mitigated nor accepted.</p>

  {% for group in remaining_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for cat in group.categories %}
  <h3 class="category-heading"><a href="#cat-{{ cat.slug }}">{{ cat.title }}</a></h3>
  <table class="compact-risk-table">
    <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Asset</th><th>Status</th></tr>
    {% for f in cat.findings %}
    <tr>
      <td class="severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | capitalize }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.risk_status | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="accepted-risks" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Severity Analysis of {{ accepted_finding_count }} Accepted Risks in {{ accepted_categories | length }} Categories</h2>
  <p>This section covers accepted risks, which are findings where the underlying vulnerability is still present in the
  architecture, but a documented decision was made to tolerate it rather than remediate it. 
  Each risk acceptance contains a justification, ticket reference, reviewer, and date.</p>
  {% if accepted_categories %}
  {% for group in accepted_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for cat in group.categories %}
  <h3 class="category-heading"><a href="#cat-{{ cat.slug }}">{{ cat.title }}</a></h3>
  <table class="compact-risk-table">
    <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Asset</th><th>Justification</th><th>Ticket</th><th>Checked By</th><th>Date</th></tr>
    {% for f in cat.findings %}
    <tr>
      <td class="severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | capitalize }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.justification or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.ticket or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.checked_by or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.closed_date or '—' }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No accepted risks in this run.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="closed-risks" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Severity Analysis of {{ closed_finding_count }} Closed Risks in {{ closed_categories | length }} Categories</h2>
  <p>This section covers closed risks, which are mitigated or false-positive findings. Here, the underlying vulnerability is either
  fixed or was never a real exposure in the first place. Each risk mitigation has a justification, reviewer, and date that come from that finding's
  <code>risk_tracking</code> entry.</p>
  {% if closed_categories %}
  {% for group in closed_domain_groups %}
  <h2 class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for cat in group.categories %}
  <h3 class="category-heading"><a href="#cat-{{ cat.slug }}">{{ cat.title }}</a></h3>
  <table class="compact-risk-table">
    <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Asset</th><th>Status</th><th>Justification</th><th>Checked By</th><th>Date</th></tr>
    {% for f in cat.findings %}
    <tr>
      <td class="severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | capitalize }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.risk_status | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.justification or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.checked_by or '—' }}</td>
      <td>{{ f.closed_date or '—' }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No closed risks in this run.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="risks-by-category" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Identified Risks by Vulnerability Category</h2>
  <p>This section identifies every risk finding detail and is categorized by its assigned security domain.</p>
  {% for group in all_domain_groups %}
  <h2 id="domain-{{ group.slug }}" class="domain-heading">{{ group.domain }}</h2>
  {% for cat in group.categories %}
  <div id="cat-{{ cat.slug }}" class="category-block">

    {% if cat.shared %}
    {# Every finding in this category agrees on STRIDE/function/CWE/action/
       mitigation -- the vulnerability class itself -- so that's shown once
       here instead of once per asset. True whenever a category has one
       finding (trivially) or several that agree; the whole section uses
       this one shape by default, with the non-uniform branch below as the
       exception rather than the rule. Severity/likelihood/impact/status
       stay broken out per asset in the table below even on categories
       where those also happen to be identical everywhere (rather than
       switching to a flat asset list there) -- a deliberate visual-
       consistency choice: every collapsed category has the same shape, so
       a reader always finds severity/likelihood/impact/status in the same
       place, right after the heading -- no severity badge on the heading
       itself, since severity is now always a table column, never
       restated in two places. Each row keeps its own finding-
       {{ synthetic_id }} anchor so the Severity Analysis / Closed Risks
       tables' jump-links still resolve. #}
    <div class="finding">
      <h3 class="category-heading">{{ cat.title }}</h3>
      <table class="finding-meta">
        <tr>
          <td><strong>STRIDE:</strong> {{ cat.shared.stride }}</td>
          <td><strong>Function:</strong> {{ cat.shared.function }}</td>
          <td><strong>CWE:</strong> {{ cat.shared.cwe }}</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <table class="compact-risk-table">
        <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Asset</th><th>Likelihood</th><th>Impact Rating</th><th>Status</th></tr>
        {% for f in cat.findings %}
        <tr id="finding-{{ f.synthetic_id }}">
          <td class="severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.exploitation_likelihood | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.exploitation_impact | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.risk_status | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</td>
        </tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </table>
      {% if cat.enrichment %}
      <p><strong>Description:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.description }}</p>
      <p><strong>Impact:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.impact }}</p>
      {% endif %}
      {% if cat.shared.action %}<p><strong>Action:</strong> {{ cat.shared.action | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.shared.mitigation %}<p><strong>Mitigation:</strong> {{ cat.shared.mitigation | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.asvs %}<p><strong>ASVS:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.asvs }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.cheat_sheet %}<p><strong>Cheat Sheet:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.cheat_sheet }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.risk_assessment %}<p><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.risk_assessment }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.false_positives %}<p><strong>False Positives:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.false_positives }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.detection_logic %}<p><strong>Detection Logic:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.detection_logic }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.references %}<p><strong>References:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.references }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    {% else %}
    {# A single-finding category is always cat.shared (see _shared_fields()'s
       docstring) so this branch is now reached only when a category has
       multiple findings that genuinely don't agree on STRIDE/function/CWE/
       action/mitigation -- a different vulnerability class per asset, not
       just different scoring. Each finding keeps its own heading -- but
       only the first carries the category-heading class (PDF bookmark +
       description/impact), rather than repeating category background
       once per asset the way the old standalone category title used to.
       Same table-first, no-badge-in-heading shape as the branch above,
       just one row per finding's own block instead of one row per asset
       inside a single shared block. #}
    {% for f in cat.findings %}
    <div id="finding-{{ f.synthetic_id }}" class="finding">
      <h3{% if loop.first %} class="category-heading"{% endif %}>{{ f.title }}</h3>
      <table class="finding-meta">
        <tr>
          <td><strong>STRIDE:</strong> {{ f.stride }}</td>
          <td><strong>Function:</strong> {{ f.function }}</td>
          <td><strong>CWE:</strong> {{ f.cwe }}</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <table class="compact-risk-table">
        <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Asset</th><th>Likelihood</th><th>Impact Rating</th><th>Status</th></tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="severity-{{ f.severity }}">{{ f.severity | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.most_relevant_technical_asset or '—' }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.exploitation_likelihood | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.exploitation_impact | capitalize }}</td>
          <td>{{ f.risk_status | replace('-', ' ') | capitalize }}</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      {% if loop.first and cat.enrichment %}
      <p><strong>Description:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.description }}</p>
      <p><strong>Impact:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.impact }}</p>
      {% endif %}
      {% if f.action %}<p><strong>Action:</strong> {{ f.action | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if f.mitigation %}<p><strong>Mitigation:</strong> {{ f.mitigation | safe }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if loop.first and cat.enrichment %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.asvs %}<p><strong>ASVS:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.asvs }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.cheat_sheet %}<p><strong>Cheat Sheet:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.cheat_sheet }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.risk_assessment %}<p><strong>Risk Assessment:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.risk_assessment }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.false_positives %}<p><strong>False Positives:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.false_positives }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.detection_logic %}<p><strong>Detection Logic:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.detection_logic }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% if cat.enrichment.references %}<p><strong>References:</strong> {{ cat.enrichment.references }}</p>{% endif %}
      {% endif %}
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- PART 3: APPLICATION COMPONENTS                                -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<section id="tag-listing" style="page: content;">
  <h1>Part III: Application Components</h1>
  <h2 class="section-heading">Tag Listing</h2>
  <p>This section lists every tag that is applied in this model, its meaning, and how often.</p>
  <table class="tag-table">
    <tr><th>Tag</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Usage Count</th></tr>
    {% for tag in tags %}
    <tr>
      <td><code>{{ tag.name }}</code></td>
      <td>{{ tag.description }}</td>
      <td>{{ tag.usage_count }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </table>
</section>

<section id="data-assets" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Data Assets</h2>
  <p>This section outlines the information the application model processes or stores, independent of which technical asset
  touches it. Each entry records who's accountable for it (<strong>Owner</strong>), roughly how
  much of it exists (<strong>Quantity</strong>), where it comes from (<strong>Origin</strong>),
  and its Confidentiality/Integrity/Availability ratings, defined below.</p>

  <h2>Confidentiality</h2>
  <table class="asset-table">
    <tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Public</td><td>No confidentiality requirement. Freely shareable.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Internal</td><td>Internal to the organization. Not for public release.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Restricted</td><td>Limited distribution within the organization. Need-to-know basis.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Confidential</td><td>Sensitive business or personal data. Restricted by policy.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Strictly-Confidential</td><td>Highest sensitivity. CUI, classified, or regulated data.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Integrity &amp; Availability</h2>
  <p>Both Integrity and Availability use the same five-level scale, applied to what happens if the asset is respectively
  modified (Integrity) or made unavailable (Availability). Technical Assets (next section) reuses
  this same scale, applied to the asset itself rather than a specific payload.</p>
  <table class="asset-table">
    <tr><th>Level</th><th>If modified (Integrity)</th><th>If unavailable (Availability)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Archive</td><td>Historical/reference data. Modification has minimal impact.</td><td>Minimal operational impact.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Operational</td><td>Operational data. Modification causes limited disruption.</td><td>Causes limited disruption.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Important</td><td>Important data. Modification causes significant disruption.</td><td>Causes significant disruption.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Critical</td><td>Critical data. Modification causes major mission impact.</td><td>Causes major mission impact.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mission-Critical</td><td>Modification causes immediate mission failure.</td><td>Causes immediate mission failure.</td></tr>
  </table>

  {% for a in data_assets %}
  <div class="asset-node">
    <h3>{{ a.title }}</h3>
    <table class="finding-meta">
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Owner:</strong> {{ a.owner }}</td>
        <td><strong>Quantity:</strong> {{ a.quantity }}</td>
        <td><strong>Origin:</strong> {{ a.origin }}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> <span class="cia-badge conf-{{ a.confidentiality }}">{{ a.confidentiality }}</span></td>
        <td><strong>Integrity:</strong> <span class="cia-badge ia-{{ a.integrity }}">{{ a.integrity }}</span></td>
        <td><strong>Availability:</strong> <span class="cia-badge ia-{{ a.availability }}">{{ a.availability }}</span></td>
      </tr>
    </table>
    {% if a.description %}<p>{{ a.description }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="technical-assets" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Technical Assets</h2>
  <p>This section depicts every component in the application model's architecture, with its own risk-relevant attributes:
  <strong>Type</strong> (external-entity, process, or datastore), <strong>Technology</strong>
  (its specific technical classification), <strong>Machine</strong> (physical, virtual,
  container, or serverless), and <strong>Encryption</strong>. Confidentiality, Integrity, and
  Availability use the same five-level scale defined in Data Assets, applied here to the asset
  itself rather than a specific payload.</p>
  <p>Relative Attacker Attractiveness (RAA) is a score Threagile computes for each asset, which
  factors in its exposure, size, and CIA ratings, and estimates how appealing a target it is
  relative to the rest of the model. Threagile's own risk rules use RAA as an input to their
  likelihood scoring, so it helps explain why some findings are rated more severely than others.</p>
  {% macro raa_badge(raa) -%}
  {%- if raa is none -%}
  —
  {%- else -%}
  {%- set band = 'critical' if raa >= 80 else 'high' if raa >= 60 else 'elevated' if raa >= 40 else 'medium' if raa >= 20 else 'low' -%}
  <span class="raa-badge raa-{{ band }}">{{ raa | round | int }}</span>
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- endmacro %}
  {% for a in technical_assets %}
  <div class="asset-node">
    <h3>{{ a.title }}</h3>
    <table class="finding-meta">
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Type:</strong> {{ a.type }}</td>
        <td><strong>Technology:</strong> {{ a.technology }}</td>
        <td><strong>Machine:</strong> {{ a.machine }}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Encryption:</strong> {{ a.encryption }}</td>
        <td><strong>Confidentiality:</strong> <span class="cia-badge conf-{{ a.confidentiality }}">{{ a.confidentiality }}</span></td>
        <td><strong>Integrity:</strong> <span class="cia-badge ia-{{ a.integrity }}">{{ a.integrity }}</span></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Availability:</strong> <span class="cia-badge ia-{{ a.availability }}">{{ a.availability }}</span></td>
        <td><strong>RAA:</strong> {{ raa_badge(a.raa) }}</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
    </table>
    {% if a.description %}<p>{{ a.description }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="out-of-scope-assets" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Out of Scope Assets</h2>
  {% if out_of_scope_assets %}
  <p>This section lists the assets that are excluded from the threat model's accreditation boundary and their
  justification for exclusion.</p>
  {% for a in out_of_scope_assets %}
  <h2>{{ a.title }}</h2>
  <p>{{ a.justification_out_of_scope }}</p>
  {% endfor %}
  {% else %}
  <p>No technical assets in this model are marked out of scope.</p>
  {% endif %}
</section>

<section id="trust-boundaries" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Trust Boundaries</h2>
  <p>This section lists every network, cluster, and namespace boundary in the model, nested to match the
  architecture, along with the associated tags and technical assets.</p>
  {% macro boundary(b, depth=0) %}
  <div class="boundary-node" style="margin-left: {{ depth * 20 }}px;">
    <h3>{{ b.title }} <span class="boundary-type">({{ b.type }})</span></h3>
    <p>{{ b.description }}</p>
    {% if b.tags %}<p class="tag-list">{% for t in b.tags %}<code>{{ t }}</code> {% endfor %}</p>{% endif %}
    {% if b.assets %}<p><strong>Assets inside:</strong> {{ b.assets | join(', ') }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% for child in b.children %}{{ boundary(child, depth + 1) }}{% endfor %}
  {% endmacro %}
  {% for root in trust_boundary_tree %}{{ boundary(root) }}{% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="shared-runtimes" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Shared Runtimes</h2>
  <p>This section contains compute platforms, such as a shared Kubernetes cluster, that host multiple technical assets
  that don't otherwise share a trust boundary. Relevant for blast-radius analysis if the platform
  itself is compromised.</p>
  {% for rt in shared_runtimes %}
  <div class="runtime-node">
    <h3>{{ rt.title }}</h3>
    <p>{{ rt.description }}</p>
    {% if rt.tags %}<p class="tag-list">{% for t in rt.tags %}<code>{{ t }}</code> {% endfor %}</p>{% endif %}
    {% if rt.assets %}<p><strong>Assets running:</strong> {{ rt.assets | join(', ') }}</p>{% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</section>

<section id="disclaimer" style="page: content;">
  <h2 class="section-heading">Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>This report was generated by an automated threat-modeling process (Threagile, augmented with
  a custom risk-injection engine) and represents a point-in-time architectural analysis based
  on the model provided. It is intended to support, not replace, manual security review, penetration
  testing, and continuous monitoring. Identified risks should be triaged and tracked to resolution
  through the organization's standard risk-management process.</p>
</section>

</body>
</html>
